"""English — details, quran_b_1 (35 questions)."""

D = {
"quran_081": "Surah An-Naba opens with a question and its answer: \"About what are they asking one another? About the great news, that over which they are in disagreement\" (78:1-3). The surah then answers the doubters with signs: the earth as a resting place, the mountains as stakes, sleep, night, day, seven heavens and a burning lamp (78:6-13). Then it states: \"Indeed, the Day of Judgement is an appointed time\" (78:17). A Muslim learns that the great news is the Day of Resurrection, and that the created world is its proof.",
"quran_082": "The surah opens with a name of the Last Day and three questions: \"The Striking Calamity! What is the Striking Calamity? And what can make you know what the Striking Calamity is?\" (101:1-3). The answer follows: \"It is the Day when people will be like moths, dispersed, and the mountains will be like wool, fluffed up\" (101:4-5). The name means the one that strikes. A Muslim learns that mountains he sees as immovable will one day scatter like wool, and prepares for that Day.",
"quran_083": "Surah Al-Qari'ah sets two outcomes side by side: \"Then as for one whose scales are heavy, he will be in a pleasant life. But as for one whose scales are light, his refuge will be an abyss\" (101:6-9). The surah then asks what the abyss is and answers: \"It is a Fire, intensely hot\" (101:10-11). The abyss has a name, Hawiyah, and it is the only refuge left to one whose good deeds weigh little. A Muslim learns that deeds are weighed, and works to make his scale heavy.",
"quran_084": "Surah At-Takathur opens: \"Competition in increase diverts you until you visit the graveyards\" (102:1-2). It names not what is amassed but the result: the chase lasts until death ends it. Then a repeated warning, \"No! You are going to know. Then no! You are going to know\" (102:3-4), and the promise that the Hellfire will be seen with certainty (102:6-7). It closes: \"Then you will surely be asked that Day about pleasure\" (102:8). A Muslim learns to hold what he gathers lightly, since every comfort will be asked about.",
"quran_085": "The surah begins: \"Woe to every scorner and mocker, who collects wealth and counts it. He thinks that his wealth will make him immortal\" (104:1-3). Mockery and hoarding sit together, as if the second could protect the first. Then: \"No! He will surely be thrown into the Crusher. And what can make you know what is the Crusher? It is the fire of Allah, kindled\" (104:4-6). A Muslim learns to guard his tongue from mockery and to spend rather than count, since wealth is no shield against death.",
"quran_086": "Surah Quraysh is one sentence: \"For the accustomed security of the Quraysh, their accustomed security in the caravan of winter and summer\" (106:1-2), \"let them worship the Lord of this House, who has fed them against hunger and made them safe from fear\" (106:3-4). The House is the one Ibrahim prayed beside when he settled his family in a valley without crops and asked Allah to provide them with fruits (14:37). A Muslim learns to trace his food and his safety back to Allah, and to answer with worship.",
"quran_087": "The question is addressed to the Prophet ﷺ: \"Did We not expand for you your breast? And We removed from you your burden, and raised high for you your repute\" (94:1-4). Then the promise is given twice: \"For indeed, with hardship will be ease. Indeed, with hardship will be ease\" (94:5-6). It ends with a task: \"So when you have finished, then stand up, and to your Lord direct your longing\" (94:7-8). A Muslim learns to count favours already given before fearing a present hardship.",
"quran_088": "The surah opens with four oaths: \"By the fig and the olive, and by Mount Sinai, and by this secure city\" (95:1-3). Mount Sinai is the mount at whose side Allah called Musa (19:52), and the secure city is Makkah, which Ibrahim asked Allah to make secure (14:35) and where the Prophet ﷺ dwelt (90:1-2). The oaths lead to their statement: \"We have certainly created man in the best of stature\" (95:4). A Muslim learns that Allah swears by places of revelation to affirm the honour of the human being.",
"quran_089": "\"We have certainly created man in the best of stature; then We return him to the lowest of the low, except for those who believe and do righteous deeds\" (95:4-6). The verse follows the surah's opening oaths and describes a gift and a risk: the fine form is given to all, and only faith and good deeds keep him from falling. It then asks, \"Is not Allah the most just of judges?\" (95:8). A Muslim learns that the honour of his creation is kept through belief and good deeds.",
"quran_090": "\"I swear by this city, and you are a dweller in this city, and by the father and that which was born\" (90:1-3). The city is Makkah, over which Ibrahim prayed, \"My Lord, make this city secure\" (14:35), and the oath joins its honour to the Prophet's ﷺ presence in it. The oath introduces a statement about every person: \"We have certainly created man into hardship\" (90:4). A Muslim learns that Makkah and the Prophet's ﷺ dwelling in it are honoured together.",
"quran_091": "Just before, the surah says of man: \"But he has not broken through the difficult pass\" (90:11). Then it explains: \"And what can make you know what is the difficult pass? It is the freeing of a slave, or feeding on a day of severe hunger an orphan of near relationship, or a needy person in misery\" (90:12-16). The climb continues with faith, and urging one another to patience and compassion (90:17). A Muslim learns that the hard road upward is walked by giving, on the day giving costs most.",
"quran_092": "\"By the night when it covers, and by the day when it appears, and by He who created the male and female, indeed, your efforts are diverse\" (92:1-4). The oaths pair opposites, and human striving also parts in two. The surah then draws the two paths: the one who gives, fears Allah and believes in the best is eased toward ease, while the one who withholds and denies the best is eased toward difficulty (92:5-10). A Muslim learns that each path grows easier by walking it, and chooses the first.",
"quran_093": "\"Exalt the name of your Lord, the Most High, who created and proportioned, and who destined and guided\" (87:1-3). The surah goes on to promise the Prophet ﷺ, \"We will make you recite, and you will not forget\" (87:6), and names who succeeds: \"He has certainly succeeded who purifies himself and mentions the name of his Lord and prays\" (87:14-15). It closes by placing this teaching in the scriptures of Ibrahim and Musa (87:18-19). A Muslim learns to begin, as the surah begins, by declaring his Lord above every fault.",
"quran_094": "\"Has there reached you the report of the Overwhelming Event?\" (88:1). The surah answers by describing two kinds of faces on that Day. Some \"will be humbled, working and exhausted\", and will enter a burning Fire (88:2-4). Others \"will show pleasure, with their effort satisfied, in an elevated garden\" (88:8-10). The event is named for covering: it comes over everyone. A Muslim learns to picture his own face on that Day and to work now for the second description.",
"quran_095": "\"Then do they not look at the camels, how they are created? And at the sky, how it is raised? And at the mountains, how they are erected? And at the earth, how it is spread out?\" (88:17-20). The four questions follow the description of the Last Day and turn the listener to what stands before his eyes. The surah then tells the Prophet ﷺ, \"So remind, you are only a reminder\" (88:21). A Muslim learns that looking closely at an ordinary creature is itself a way to faith.",
"quran_096": "\"By the sky containing great stars, and by the promised Day, and by the witness and what is witnessed\" (85:1-3). The oaths open onto a story: \"Cursed were the companions of the trench, the fire full of fuel, when they were sitting near it\" (85:4-6). The believers were punished only because they believed in Allah, the Exalted in Might, the Praiseworthy (85:8). The surah promises the persecutors Hell and the believers gardens beneath which rivers flow (85:10-11). A Muslim learns that Allah sees every wrong done to the faithful.",
"quran_097": "\"By the sky and the night comer, and what can make you know what the night comer is? It is the piercing star\" (86:1-3). The oath leads to its point: \"There is no soul but that it has over it a protector\" (86:4). The surah turns to the human being, asking him to consider what he was created from, and affirms that Allah can bring him back (86:5-8). A Muslim learns that as the star pierces the dark, nothing about him is hidden from the One who guards him.",
"quran_098": "\"When the earth is shaken with its final earthquake, and the earth discharges its burdens, and man says, 'What is wrong with it?' That Day, it will report its news, because your Lord has commanded it\" (99:1-5). The earth does three things it has never done: it shakes to its end, empties what it holds, and speaks. Then \"the people will depart separated into categories to be shown their deeds\" (99:6). A Muslim learns that the ground he walks on will one day be a witness.",
"quran_099": "\"So whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it\" (99:7-8). These are the last words of Surah Az-Zalzalah, which begins with the earth shaken and telling its news and continues with people coming forth in groups to be shown their deeds (99:1-6). The measure is the smallest thing the listener can picture, and it applies in both directions. A Muslim learns not to belittle a small kindness or a small wrong, since both will be seen.",
"quran_100": "\"By the racers, panting, and the producers of sparks when striking, and the chargers at dawn, stirring up thereby clouds of dust, arriving thereby in the center collectively\" (100:1-5). The five verses picture horses charging, hooves striking sparks from stone, a raid at dawn, and the press into the enemy's midst. The oath supports a verdict on the rider: \"Indeed mankind, to his Lord, is ungrateful, and indeed, he is to that a witness\" (100:6-7). A Muslim learns that even his finest strength can be spent in ingratitude.",
"quran_101": "\"This is the Book about which there is no doubt, a guidance for those conscious of Allah\" (2:2) follows the letters Alif, Lam, Meem (2:1). The next verses describe those people: they believe in the unseen, establish the prayer, spend from what they are given, and are certain of the Hereafter (2:3-4). Of them the surah says, \"Those are upon guidance from their Lord, and it is those who are the successful\" (2:5). A Muslim learns that guidance is offered to all but received by the God-conscious.",
"quran_102": "\"Indeed, it is We who sent down the Reminder, and indeed, We will be its guardian\" (15:9). The promise answers mockery reported two verses earlier, where the deniers said to the Prophet ﷺ, \"O you upon whom the Reminder has been sent down, indeed you are mad; why do you not bring us the angels?\" (15:6-7). The reply is not a defence of the Messenger but a guarantee for the message: Allah Himself keeps it. A Muslim learns that the Qur'an he reads is kept safe by its Sender.",
"quran_103": "\"Then do they not reflect upon the Qur'an? If it had been from other than Allah, they would have found within it much contradiction\" (4:82). The verse comes in a passage about those who say \"obedience\" to the Prophet ﷺ and then plan otherwise at night (4:81), just after the statement that whoever obeys the Messenger has obeyed Allah (4:80). The argument invites examination rather than blind acceptance: read closely, and the Book's consistency shows its origin. A Muslim learns that reflecting on the Qur'an is asked of him.",
"quran_104": "\"If We had sent down this Qur'an upon a mountain, you would have seen it humbled and coming apart from fear of Allah. And these examples We present to the people so that they may give thought\" (59:21). The verses after it name Allah the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, the King, the Holy, the Peace (59:22-23). The picture is a rock splitting where a human heart stays unmoved. A Muslim learns to let the Qur'an weigh on him as much as it would on stone.",
"quran_105": "The command opens the surah, addressed to the Prophet ﷺ wrapped in his garments: he is told to rise in the night, half of it or a little less or more (73:2-3), \"and recite the Qur'an with measured recitation\" (73:4). The reason follows: \"Indeed, We will cast upon you a heavy word\" (73:5), and the hours of the night are more suitable for words (73:6). Tartil is slow, clear reading that lets each word land. A Muslim learns to recite without hurry, giving the Qur'an the pace its weight deserves.",
"quran_106": "\"So when the Qur'an is recited, listen to it and pay attention, that you may receive mercy\" (7:204). The verse follows the Prophet's ﷺ words, \"I only follow what is revealed to me from my Lord; this is enlightenment from your Lord, and guidance and mercy\" (7:203). The next verse turns the quiet inward: remember your Lord within yourself, in humility and fear, and do not be among the heedless (7:205). A Muslim learns that receiving the Qur'an begins with silence, and that listening is a door to mercy.",
"quran_107": "\"So when you recite the Qur'an, seek refuge in Allah from Satan, the expelled\" (16:98). The next verses explain why the refuge works: \"Indeed, there is for him no authority over those who have believed and rely upon their Lord. His authority is only over those who take him as an ally\" (16:99-100). The reader answers by saying the words the verse gives: I seek refuge in Allah from Satan, the expelled. A Muslim learns to open his reading under Allah's protection, so nothing comes between him and the words.",
"quran_108": "\"This is a blessed Book which We have revealed to you, that they might reflect upon its verses and that those of understanding would be reminded\" (38:29). The verse sits between a question and a story: before it, Allah asks whether He would treat those who believe and do good like those who spread corruption (38:28), and after it the surah turns to Dawud and Sulayman (38:30). Reflection and reminder are the two purposes named. A Muslim learns that reading without thinking misses the reason the Qur'an was sent.",
"quran_109": "\"Say: If mankind and the jinn gathered in order to produce the like of this Qur'an, they could not produce the like of it, even if they were to each other assistants\" (17:88). The verse follows a reminder that Allah could take away what He had revealed (17:86), and that the revelation is a mercy from Him (17:87). Most people, the next verse adds, refuse all but disbelief (17:89). A Muslim learns that the Qur'an's challenge stands open, and that its being unmatched shows where it came from.",
"quran_110": "\"So high above all is Allah, the Sovereign, the Truth. And do not hasten with the Qur'an before its revelation is completed to you, and say: My Lord, increase me in knowledge\" (20:114). The verse is addressed to the Prophet ﷺ while he was receiving the Qur'an, and joins patience in receiving revelation with a prayer for more. The request is not for wealth, power or ease, but for knowledge alone. A Muslim learns to make this his daily prayer, and to receive knowledge slowly and completely.",
"quran_111": "\"Allah has sent down the best statement: a consistent Book wherein is reiteration. The skins shiver therefrom of those who fear their Lord; then their skins and their hearts relax at the remembrance of Allah\" (39:23). It comes after the surah contrasts the one whose breast Allah has opened to Islam with the hard-hearted (39:22). It describes a listener, not a page: the Qur'an first shakes, then softens. A Muslim learns that a shiver at a verse and the calm after it mark a heart that fears its Lord.",
"quran_112": "The Qur'an presents the example: \"a good word is like a good tree, whose root is firmly fixed and its branches in the sky; it produces its fruit all the time, by permission of its Lord\" (14:24-25). The next verse gives the opposite: a bad tree, uprooted, with no stability (14:26). Allah then promises to keep believers firm with the firm word (14:27). A Muslim learns that the word of faith he holds is meant to root deep and bear fruit in every season.",
"quran_113": "The comparison starts with rain: \"He sends down from the sky rain, and valleys flow according to their capacity, and the torrent carries a rising foam\" (13:17). The verse adds the foam of heated metal, then the lesson: \"Thus Allah presents truth and falsehood. As for the foam, it vanishes, cast off; but as for that which benefits the people, it remains on the earth\" (13:17). Falsehood rises and is gone; truth stays. A Muslim learns not to be impressed by what floats highest, but to seek what lasts.",
"quran_114": "\"Those who have believed and whose hearts are assured by the remembrance of Allah. Unquestionably, by the remembrance of Allah hearts are assured\" (13:28). The verse describes the people Allah guides, those who turn back to Him (13:27), and the next verse promises them \"a good state and a good return\" (13:29). The statement is made twice in one verse, first as a description and then as a rule. A Muslim learns that when his heart is unsettled, the remedy the Qur'an names is remembering Allah.",
"quran_115": "\"Say: If the sea were ink for the words of my Lord, the sea would be exhausted before the words of my Lord were exhausted\" (18:109). The verse comes at the end of Surah Al-Kahf, whose last verse says the Prophet ﷺ is only a man like others, and whoever hopes to meet his Lord should do righteous work (18:110). The sea has a bottom; the words of Allah do not. A Muslim learns that the Qur'an he holds is a drop from a sea that never runs dry.",
}
